# Exercise: Serial Baseline
#
# Amdahl's Law: Speedup = 1 / ((1-p) + p/n)
#   p = fraction of work that can be parallelized
#   n = number of workers
#
# Before measuring speedup, we need a baseline. Time a serial
# task: 3 items processed sequentially (10ms each). Elapsed
# should be at least 30ms (3 × 10ms).
#
# Your task: receive 3 results, then compute elapsed = now - start.
#
# Stack hint: the `start` timestamp is stashed on the aux stack
# with `>aux` so it's out of the way while you juggle the work
# and results channels. Pull it back with `aux>` once the work
# is done.
#
# When done, delete the marker line below.

# audit:allow(stub-lint-error)
# I AM NOT DONE

: test-serial-baseline ( -- )
    chan.make   # work
    chan.make   # results

    # 1 worker processes 3 items serially. Closure captures
    # ( work results ); `over` copies the work channel each
    # iteration so it survives the receive.
    [
        over chan.receive drop 10 time.sleep-ms 1 i.+ over chan.send drop
        over chan.receive drop 10 time.sleep-ms 1 i.+ over chan.send drop
        over chan.receive drop 10 time.sleep-ms 1 i.+ over chan.send drop
        drop drop
    ] strand.spawn drop
    # Stack: ( work results )

    time.now >aux   # stash start time on the aux stack

    # Send 3 items into work
    over 1 swap chan.send drop
    over 1 swap chan.send drop
    over 1 swap chan.send drop

    # Your code here:
    # 1. Receive 3 results (drop value and flag each time).
    # 2. Drop the work + results channels.
    # 3. Push `time.now`, pull `start` back with `aux>`,
    #    compute elapsed with `i.-`.
    drop drop 0

    # Do not edit below this line
    # Serial: 3 items x 10ms = at least 30ms
    30 >= test.assert
;
